==========================================================================
ADVERSARIAL PROBES — EP-AEC draft-02, independent implementation
==========================================================================

--- P1: label/type namespace collision in the satisfied set ---
      honest chain (1 delegation leg, no quorum) : DENY
      attacker chain (same leg, label='ep-quorum'): ALLOW

[P1] Presenter-controlled label satisfies a required component TYPE
      -> ATTACK SUCCEEDS -- finding
      Requirement 'delegation AND ep-quorum' has NO human leg in the attacker chain; the only difference is a label string. VERDICT: honest=DENY attacker=ALLOW. Cause: §4 step 3.4 puts type and label into one satisfied set, and §5 resolves IDENT against both.

--- P2: cross-binding defense depends on component formats signing the action ---
      ONE signature, small action (3 units)      : ALLOW
      ONE signature, BIG action (3,000,000 units): ALLOW

[P2] Cross-binding defense is only as strong as each component's signature coverage
      -> ATTACK SUCCEEDS -- finding
      A component format that omits the action binding from its signed body lets ONE valid signature authorize TWO different actions -- both chains return ALLOW. EP-AEC's core defense (§4 step 3.3) is therefore conditional on a property the spec explicitly declines to define (§1.1). Architectural boundary, not an implementation bug -- but 'EP-AEC conformant' alone does NOT imply cross-binding safety, and the draft should say so in Security Considerations.

--- P3: §5 IDENT cannot express labels containing spaces ---
      requirement 'two-person human authorization' -> RequirementSyntaxError: trailing tokens at position 1: ['human', 'authorization']

[P3] §3's own example label is unreferenceable by §5's grammar
      -> ATTACK SUCCEEDS -- finding
      §3 shows label="two-person human authorization". §5's grammar defines IDENT as one token and the expression tokenises on whitespace, so that label can never appear in a requirement. Labels are usable only if they contain no spaces. Spec inconsistency; fix is to quote or escape IDENT.

--- P4: the left-to-right precedence trap ---
      'a OR b AND c' over {'a'}  conformant(LTR)=False   naive(precedence)=True

[P4] Conformant left-to-right evaluation diverges from conventional precedence
      -> ATTACK SUCCEEDS -- finding
      Same expression, same satisfied set, opposite verdicts. This is a deliberate spec choice, and it is the single most likely interoperability failure for independent implementations. Recommend the draft publish this exact vector.

